{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ba8b6f78b79a667b", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tVoxSumm: A Multilingual Corpus of Long-Form Spoken News for Joint\n2\tSummarization and Translation\n3\tYejin Jeon1,2† , Marie Maltais1,2† , Virginia Ceccatelli1,2 , Min Ma3 , David Ifeoluwa Adelani1,2,4\n4\t1\n5\tMila - Quebec AI Institute 2 McGill University, Canada\n6\t3\n7\tGoogle DeepMind 4 Canada CIFAR AI Chair\n8\t\n9\tarXiv:2608.10359v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n10\t\n11\tAbstract\n12\tAs information increasingly traverses linguistic\n13\tboundaries, users require concise cross-lingual\n14\trepresentations of long-form content. Nevertheless, long-document summarization research remains text-centric, whereas multilingual speech\n15\tresearch has largely prioritized translation, preserving source content rather than compressing it. We address this methodological gap\n16\tby formalizing joint speech summarization\n17\tand translation (JSumT): the generation of\n18\ta succinct, faithful target-language summary\n19\tdirectly from a long spoken document in a\n20\tsource language. We additionally introduce\n21\tVOX S UMM, the first multilingual and crosslingual benchmark for this task, comprising\n22\t10,045 BBC article-summary pairs across 24\n23\tlanguages and encompassing approximately\n24\t703 hours of speech data. Our evaluation of\n25\trepresentative speech-language models reveals\n26\tpronounced variation across models and generation settings: Gemini3.1-Pro demonstrates the\n27\tgreatest consistency, summarization into English generally surpasses generation into nonEnglish target languages, and translating an\n28\tentire document before summarization compounds instruction-following failures. Through\n29\tthe release of VOX S UMM, we establish a foundation for developing and evaluating multilingual systems capable of jointly interpreting,\n30\tcompressing, and translating long-form speech.\n31\t\n32\t1\n33\t\n34\tIntroduction\n35\t\n36\tSpoken language has emerged as a primary\n37\tmedium for communication and information consumption. With the rapid expansion of digital\n38\tmedia, the availability of such spoken content\n39\thas grown substantially, while individual recordings have become increasingly longer and more\n40\tinformation-rich. This abundance creates significant challenges for listeners who must efficiently\n41\t† denotes equal contribution.\n42\t\n43\tcomprehend, identify, and retain the most relevant information from long-form spoken contents (Clifton et al., 2020; Retkowski et al., 2025).\n44\tSpeech summarization aims to address this challenge by transforming extended spoken recordings\n45\tinto concise overview of an article that preserve\n46\ttheir most essential content.\n47\tDespite its practical significance, long-form\n48\tspeech summarization has received substantially\n49\tless attention than its text-based counterpart\n50\t(Retkowski et al., 2025). It is important to note\n51\tthat long-form speech summarization is not merely\n52\ttext summarization applied to a different input\n53\tmodality. Rather, spoken language conveys information through both lexical content and paralinguistic signals, including prosody, emphasis,\n54\thesitation patterns, pronunciation variation, and\n55\tdisfluencies. These acoustic cues can meaningfully\n56\tinfluence how information is interpreted and summarized. In fact, access to the original speech signal, rather than a transcript alone, has been shown\n57\tto affect both content selection and factual consistency in summary generation (Sharma et al.,\n58\t2024b). Beyond these representational differences,\n59\tlong-form speech introduces significant computational challenges. Acoustic sequences are substantially longer than their textual counterparts, resulting in increased memory requirements and greater\n60\ttraining and inference complexity, thereby complicating both model development and evaluation\n61\t(Kano et al., 2023; Sharma et al., 2024a).\n62\tBeyond the challenges associated with the\n63\tspeech modality, existing research is also constrained by the limited availability of resources that\n64\tsupport broad linguistic diversity. Although realworld data has become increasingly multilingual,\n65\tsummarization research remains largely concentrated on a small subset of high-resource languages,\n66\tparticularly English (Chang et al., 2024). While\n67\tthere are multilingual summarization benchmarks\n68\tsuch as XL-Sum (Hasan et al., 2021) and Cross-\n69\t\n70\t\fSum (Bhattacharjee et al., 2023), which provide\n71\textensive language coverage, these are restricted\n72\tto textual domains. Meanwhile, speech-based\n73\tmultilingual datasets such as MuST-C (Di Gangi\n74\tet al., 2019), CoVoST 2 (Wang et al., 2021), and\n75\tFleurs (Conneau et al., 2022) primarily target\n76\tspeech translation, where the objective is to preserve the complete semantic content of the source\n77\tutterance rather than identify and compress its most\n78\tsalient information. As a result, existing resources\n79\tdo not enable the evaluation of whether a model can\n80\tcomprehend long spoken documents, extract their\n81\tkey information, and generate concise summaries\n82\tacross languages.\n83\tTo address these gaps, we formalize the joint\n84\tspeech summarization and translation (JSumT)\n85\ttask, which requires a model to generate a faithful\n86\tand concise summary in a target language when\n87\tgiven a long spoken document in a source language.\n88\tThis setting captures a realistic scenario in which\n89\tusers consume spoken content produced in one\n90\tlanguage but require condensed information in another. To support systematic evaluation for the\n91\tJSumT task, we present VOX S UMM, a multilingual long-form speech summarization benchmark\n92\tthat is constructed from long-form BBC text-based\n93\tnews articles and their cross-lingual summaries.\n94\tSpecifically, VOX S UMM contains approximately\n95\t703 hours of 10,045 article-summary pairs spanning 24 languages. Using this benchmark, we investigate representative speech language models, and\n96\tthe impact of different methodologies, including\n97\tzero-shot, few-shot, and Chain-of-Thought (CoT)based prompting. Finally, to facilitate future multilingual speech summarization research, we release\n98\tthe VOX S UMM dataset and accompanying code.\n99\t\n100\t2\n101\t\n102\tRelated Work\n103\t\n104\tSummarization research has primarily developed\n105\tin the text domain, progressing from sentencelevel benchmarks such as CNN/DailyMail (Hermann et al., 2015) and XSum (Narayan et al.,\n106\t2018) to long-context and multilingual settings.\n107\tRecent studies have explored long-document summarization through long-context language models\n108\tand hierarchical architectures (Chang et al., 2024),\n109\twhile multilingual benchmarks such as MLSUM\n110\t(Scialom et al., 2020), XL-Sum (Hasan et al., 2021),\n111\tWikiLingua (Ladhak et al., 2020), and CrossSum\n112\t(Bhattacharjee et al., 2023) have enabled crosslingual summarization through large-scale articlesummary collections. However, these resources\n113\t\n114\tare limited to textual inputs and do not address the\n115\tchallenges of long-form speech summarization.\n116\tSpeech introduces unique challenges, including\n117\tthe absence of explicit structural cues such as punctuation and paragraph boundaries and the presence\n118\tof paralinguistic information that is often lost in\n119\tASR-based processing (Rehbein et al., 2020; Zechner and Waibel, 2000; Sharma et al., 2024b). Multilingual spoken language research has therefore\n120\tfocused primarily on speech translation, with approaches ranging from cascaded ASR–translation\n121\tpipelines to speech-to-unit and end-to-end systems\n122\t(Dong et al., 2023; Pu et al., 2025; Communication\n123\tet al., 2023; Team et al., 2026).\n124\tIn contrast, speech summarization aims to extract and compress salient information from spoken\n125\tdocuments. Prior studies have explored multimodal\n126\tsummarization (Palaskar et al., 2019), meeting summarization (Zhong et al., 2021), efficient modeling\n127\tof long speech sequences (Kano et al., 2023), and\n128\trelevance-based content selection (Sharma et al.,\n129\t2024a). Recent work further shows that speechbased summarization differs from transcript-based\n130\tsummarization in information selection and factual\n131\tconsistency, highlighting the importance of modeling speech beyond transcription (Sharma et al.,\n132\t2024b). Nevertheless, existing speech summarization resources remain largely monolingual, while\n133\tmultilingual speech benchmarks focus predominantly on translation rather than compression. We\n134\taddress this gap by introducing the joint speech\n135\tsummarization and translation (JSumT) task and\n136\tVOX S UMM, a benchmark for multilingual and\n137\tcross-lingual long-form speech summarization.\n138\t\n139\t3\n140\t\n141\tVoxSumm Dataset\n142\t\n143\tWe construct VOX S UMM as an English-centric multilingual benchmark for long-form speech summarization across languages. The construction\n144\tpipeline consists of three stages: (1) collecting\n145\tcross-lingual article-summary pairs, (2) synthesizing both source articles and target summaries into\n146\tspeech, and (3) validating the generated speech\n147\tthrough automatic and human evaluation.\n148\t3.1 Multilingual Article–Summary Collection\n149\tThe textual foundation of VOX S UMM is derived\n150\tfrom CC BY-NCSA4.0 CrossSum (Bhattacharjee\n151\tet al., 2023), a large-scale cross-lingual summarization dataset built by aligning monolingual articlesummary pairs from XL-Sum (Hasan et al., 2021)\n152\tacross languages. Specifically, CrossSum identifies articles reporting on the same underlying event\n153\t\n154\t\fLanguage\n155\t\n156\tHours\n157\t\n158\tCER (↓)\n159\t\n160\tNISQA (↑)\n161\t\n162\tAmharic\n163\tArabic\n164\tBengali\n165\tChinese\n166\tFrench\n167\