51\t\n1552\t0.647\n1553\t\n1554\t0.657\n1555\t\n1556\t(0.411)\n1557\t\n1558\t(0.302)\n1559\t\n1560\t(0.305)\n1561\t\n1562\t(0.339)\n1563\t\n1564\t(0.233)\n1565\t\n1566\t(0.229)\n1567\t\n1568\t(0.210)\n1569\t\n1570\t(0.224)\n1571\t\n1572\t(0.310)\n1573\t\n1574\t(0.240)\n1575\t\n1576\t(0.200)\n1577\t\n1578\t(0.250)\n1579\t\n1580\tTable 2: Summarization→Translation direction results. 24 English→XX language pairs are evaluated across\n1581\tFew-shot (FS), Zero-Shot (ZS), and Chain-of-Thoughts ZS (CoT) settings using Gemini3.1-Pro, Qwen3-Omni and\n1582\tGemma4-12B LLMs. Both BERTScore (top) and X-Comet-XL (bottom, in parentheses) metrics are reported.\n1583\t\n1584\tscripts. Consecutive sentences are then grouped\n1585\tinto chunks of up to 1,200 characters. Each chunk\n1586\tis synthesized independently, resampled to a common sampling rate where necessary, and concatenated in its original order to yield a single longform recording at 24 kHz. As a final quality-control\n1587\tstep, we discard any language whose synthesized\n1588\tspeech yields a CER above 25 when transcribed,\n1589\tensuring that only languages with reliably intelligible synthesis are retained in the final benchmark.\n1590\tThe resulting dataset contains approximately 29\n1591\thours of audio per language on average (Table 1).\n1592\t\n1593\tintelligibility, and naturalness.\n1594\tAfter full dataset generation, we compute\n1595\tNISQA (Mittag et al., 2021)1 to automatically assess perceived naturalness, yielding an average\n1596\tscore of 4.39 (Table 1). To complement this, 30\n1597\texternal annotators separately rate whether the synthesized article and summary audios coherently\n1598\tconvey their intended content, using a 1~5 Likert\n1599\tscale.2 As shown in Figure 1, audios receive an\n1600\taverage rating of 4.07, consistent with the NISQA\n1601\tresults and confirming that the synthesized speech\n1602\tis perceived as natural and intelligible.\n1603\t\n1604\t3.2\n1605\t\n1606\t4\n1607\t\n1608\tSpeech Quality and Human Evaluation\n1609\t\n1610\tWe validate synthesized speech quality at two\n1611\tstages: during generation and after the full dataset\n1612\tis complete. During generation, we first verify\n1613\tthat every waveform is structurally valid (e.g., nonempty, correctly encoded, and of expected duration). A fluent or native speaker of each language\n1614\tthen manually reviews a 20% sample of the generated files per direction, checking for audio quality,\n1615\t\n1616\tExperimental Setup\n1617\t\n1618\t4.1 Models\n1619\tThree representative speech-capable large language\n1620\tmodels (LLMs) across a diverse range of parameter scales are selected to evaluate JSumT:\n1621\t1\n1622\t\n1623\thttps://github.com/Lightning-AI/torchmetrics/\n1624\tblob/master/src/torchmetrics/audio/nisqa.py\n1625\t2\n1626\tDue to annotation cost, this external coherence validation\n1627\tis conducted on a subset of 10 of the 24 languages.\n1628\t\n1629\t\fFigure 2: Low-resource versus high-resource language comparisons. BERTScore-F1 results are pooled across\n1630\tFS, ZS and CoT settings for each of our models.\n1631\t\n1632\tedge-deployable open-weight Gemma4-12B, midweight Qwen3-Omni (30B), and proprietary\n1633\tGemini3.1-Pro. To examine the effects of contextual supervision and explicit reasoning, each\n1634\tmodel is evaluated under three prompting settings: zero-shot, five-shot, and CoT. The complete\n1635\tprompts are provided in Appendix D. For all experiments, Gemini3.1-Pro is evaluated using its default high-thinking configuration with a maximum\n1636\tgeneration length of 256 tokens. Gemma4-12B and\n1637\tQwen3-Omni are evaluated with a batch size of 8\n1638\tand a maximum generation length of 256 tokens.\n1639\tZero-Shot (ZS) and Few-Shot (FS) Prompting\n1640\tFor both zero-shot and few-shot evaluation, models\n1641\tare given a source-language spoken news article\n1642\tand tasked with generating a summary in the target\n1643\tlanguage. We adopt a cascaded prompting strategy in which models first summarize the source\n1644\tcontent and then translate the resulting summary\n1645\tinto the target language (or vice versa). Evaluation\n1646\tis performed on 200 held-out examples for each\n1647\tlanguage direction. For few-shot, models are additionally provided with five demonstration examples\n1648\tcomprising source-language audio articles and their\n1649\tcorresponding target-language summaries. All example samples are selected from a non-overlapping\n1650\tpool and are excluded from the evaluation split.\n1651\tCoT Reasoning In order to investigate whether\n1652\tstructured reasoning improves performance, we additionally evaluate a CoT prompting strategy, as\n1653\tin Chen et al. (2025) and (Yuan and Zhang, 2026).\n1654\tMotivated by prior work on decomposed reasoning (Wang et al., 2023), models are guided through\n1655\ttwo implicit reasoning stages before generating the\n1656\tfinal output. First, the model identifies the key\n1657\tinformational content of the spoken document using a 5W1H framework (who, what, when, where,\n1658\twhy, and how), extracting salient entities, events,\n1659\tand their temporal, spatial, causal, and procedu-\n1660\t\n1661\tral relationships. Second, the model synthesizes\n1662\tthese elements into a coherent semantic representation, determines the information most central to the\n1663\tdocument’s meaning, and expresses the resulting\n1664\tone-sentence target-language summary. Details of\n1665\tthe CoT prompt is available in Appendix D\n1666\t4.2\n1667\t\n1668\tEvaluation\n1669\t\n1670\tObjective Metrics: Since the task involves jointly\n1671\tsummarizing and translating a source-language article into a target-language summary, evaluation\n1672\tmust capture both the fidelity of the summarized\n1673\tcontent and the quality of the cross-lingual generation. Thus, summarization outputs are assessed\n1674\tusing BERTScore-F1 (Chhibbar and Kalita, 2024;\n1675\tAbdulreda Kadhim et al., 2025), while xCOMETXL (Guerreiro et al., 2024) reflects translation quality. Specifically, BERTScore-F1 computes tokenlevel semantic similarity between each predicted\n1676\tsummary and its reference (Zhang et al., 2019).\n1677\tIt is thus well suited to capturing meaning-level\n1678\toverlap that surface-level lexical metrics tend to\n1679\tmiss, which is a property especially important in\n1680\tour joint summarization-translation setting, where\n1681\toutputs must be assessed across both content and\n1682\tlanguage. See Appendix A.1 for details.\n1683\tFollowing prior cross-lingual evaluation work\n1684\t(Adelani et al., 2026), we report xCOMET-XL\n1685\t(Guerreiro et al., 2024) to evaluate translation.\n1686\tWhile xCOMET-XL is conventionally computed\n1687\tusing the source text, reference, and prediction, we\n1688\tadopt its quality-estimation (QE) configuration3 by\n1689\tscoring the predicted summary directly against the\n1690\treference without access to the source article. This\n1691\tisolates the semantic and translation-quality alignment between the model’s output and the targetlanguage reference, independent of source-side\n1692\tvariation introduced by the summarization step.\n1693\t3\n1694\t\n1695\tSee Appendix A.1.\n1696\t\n1697\t\f(a) BERTScore F1\n1698\t\n1699\t(b) xCOMET\n1700\t\n1701\tFigure 3: Correlation between Human evaluation and individual metrics. Pearson correlation reported.\n1702\t\n1703\tEng→XX\n1704\tGemini3.1-Pro FS\n1705\tGemini3.1-Pro ZS\n1706\tGemini3.1-Pro CoT\n1707\tGemma4-12B FS\n1708\tGemma4-12B ZS\n1709\tGemma4-12B CoT\n1710\tQwen3-Omni FS\n1711\tQwen3-Omni ZS\n1712\tQwen3-Omni CoT\n1713\tAvg\n1714\t\n1715\tXX→Eng\n1716\t\n1717\t0.724\n1718\t\n1719\t0.730\n1720\t\n1721\t(-0.003)\n1722\t\n1723\t(+0.005)\n1724\t\n1725\t0.686\n1726\t\n1727\t0.692\n1728\t\n1729\t(-0.005)\n1730\t\n1731\t(-0.001)\n1732\t\n1733\t0.692\n1734\t\n1735\t0.693\n1736\t\n1737\t0.580\n1738\t\n1739\t0.621\n1740\t\n1741\t(-0.032)\n1742\t\n1743\t(-0.006)\n1744\t\n1745\t0.600\n1746\t\n1747\t0.630\n1748\t\n1749\t(-0.019)\n1750\t\n1751\t(+0.005)\n1752\t\n1753\t0.619\n1754\t\n1755\t0.630\n1756\t\n1757\t0.668\n1758\t\n1759\t0.687\n1760\t\n1761\t(+0.002)\n1762\t\n1763\t(+0.000)\n1764\t\n1765\t0.211\n1766\t\n1767\t0.529\n1768\t\n1769\t(-0.448)\n1770\t\n1771\t(-0.071)\n1772\t\n1773\t0.647\n1774\t\n1775\t0.661\n1776\t\n1777\t0.578\n1778\t\n1779\t0.648\n1780\t\n1781\t(-0.081)\n1782\t\n1783\t(-0.012)\n1784\t\n1785\tTable 3: Translation→Summarization results averaged across all languages. Values in parentheses show the difference relative to the opposite\n1786\tSummarization→Translation direction. (Green =\n1787\tTranslation→Summarization is better, red = lower.)\n1788\t\n1789\tSubjective Metrics To assess multilingual summarization quality, we conduct human evaluations\n1790\tspanning a diverse range of languages: 5 highresource (French, Spanish, Portuguese, Korean,\n1791\tand Chinese) and 5 mid/low-resource (Amharic,\n1792\tGujarati, Tamil, Swahili, and Ukrainian), in both\n1793\tdirections (XX→English, English→XX). For each\n1794\tlanguage, three bilingual annotators independently\n1795\tcomplete a survey in which they listen to 50 audio articles (25 per direction), each paired with its\n1796\tsource-text article and a set of anonymized, modelwise candidate summaries. Annotators first listen\n1797\t\n1798\tFigure 4: Influence of language direction on performance in the Summarization→Translation setting.\n1799\t\n1800\tto the full audio article, then rate each candidate\n1801\tsummary on quality on a 1-to-5 scale. In the survey, a high quality summary is defined as accurate,\n1802\tengaging and informative (Appendix B).\n1803\t\n1804\t5\n1805\t\n1806\tResults\n1807\t\n1808\tTable 2 reveals systematic differences across both\n1809\tmodels and prompting strategies. Gemini3.1-Pro\n1810\tattains the strongest average BERTScore of 0.703,\n1811\tfollowed by Qwen3-Omni (0.657) and Gemma4-12B\n1812\t(0.617). With respect to prompting methodology, FS prompting proves particularly effective\n1813\tfor Gemini3.1-Pro, reaching 0.727 and outperforming its ZS and CoT variants by approximately\n1814\t0.036. Qwen3-Omni exhibits a similar, though\n1815\tmore modest, advantage under FS prompting,\n1816\twhereas Gemma4-12B remains comparatively insensitive to prompting strategy altogether. This modellevel disparity is further reflected at the language\n1817\tlevel: both Gemma4-12B and Qwen3-Omni exhibit\n1818\tmarkedly greater performance variation between\n1819\tlow- (e.g. Amharic, Gujarati, Kyrgyz) and highresource languages (e.g. Chinese, French, Spanish)\n1820\t\n1821\t\fFigure 5: Human evaluation for summary quality.\n1822\tQuality is evaluated on a Likert 1-5 scale for each model,\n1823\tsetting (CoT and Few-Shot) and language.\n1824\t\n1825\tthan Gemini3.1-Pro, which performs comparably\n1826\tacross resource settings (Figure 2). Nonetheless,\n1827\taverage performance consistently improves with\n1828\tlanguage resource availability for all models.\n1829\tThese trends hold irrespective of the evaluation\n1830\tmetric used: both BERTScore and xCOMET consistently rank Gemini3.1-Pro above Qwen3-Omni,\n1831\twhich in turn outperforms Gemma4-12B. For\n1832\tGemini3.1-Pro and Qwen3-Omni specifically,\n1833\tboth metrics further agree that FS prompting outperforms CoT, which in turn outperforms ZS. Crucially, these automatic metrics correlate strongly\n1834\twith human judgment, lending further credibility\n1835\tto the observed trends: the overall Pearson correlation for BertScore and xCOMET is 0.77 and 0.66,\n1836\trespectively (Figures 3a, 3b).\n1837\tEffect of Task Direction Table 2 prompts models to summarize and translate jointly in a single\n1838\tpass. To isolate the effect of task ordering, we\n1839\tinstead prompt models to first translate the full\n1840\taudio article into the target language, and only\n1841\tthen generate a one-sentence summary of that\n1842\ttranslation. As shown in Table 3, this reordering produces only a minimal drop in the overall average performance across all models, but\n1843\tthe effect is asymmetric across language directions: English→XX is affected substantially more\n1844\t(-0.081) than XX→English (-0.012). We attribute\n1845\tthis asymmetry to instruction-following failures\n1846\tspecific to the translate-first ordering: after translating a long audio document, models more frequently\n1847\tfail to complete the remaining instruction, in some\n1848\tcases omitting the summary entirely or hallucinating toward the end of the translation. This effect is\n1849\tmost severe for Qwen3-Omni under ZS prompting\n1850\t(English→XX: -0.448 vs. XX→English: -0.071),\n1851\t\n1852\tFigure 6: Sentence length analysis between reference and predicted summaries. We focus on the\n1853\tSummarization→Translation direction. Results are\n1854\tpooled across methods for each model.\n1855\t\n1856\tsuggesting that translate-then-summarize pipelines\n1857\tare less capable prompting regimes.\n1858\tEffect of Language Direction. Independent of\n1859\ttask ordering, we further find that the direction\n1860\tof language itself affects performance.\n1861\tAs\n1862\tseen in Table 3 and Figure 4, generating an\n1863\tEnglish summary from non-English speech\n1864\t(XX→Eng) almost always outperforms generating a non-English summary from English\n1865\tspeech (Eng→XX) across all models and both\n1866\ttask orderings (Summarization↔Translation).\n1867\tSpecifically, while language direction has little\n1868\teffect on Gemini3.1-Pro regardless of task\n1869\tordering (with only a marginally larger gap\n1870\tunder Translation→Summarization), Gemma4-12B\n1871\tand Qwen3-Omni are considerably more sensitive to language direction when the task\n1872\tis ordered as Translation→Summarization.\n1873\tAveraged across prompting methods, the\n1874\tEng→XX/XX→Eng relative difference is\n1875\t0.012 under Summarization→Translation versus 0.027 under Translation→Summarization\n1876\tfor Gemma4-12B, and 0.031 versus 0.0117 for\n1877\tQwen3-Omni.\n1878\tThis pattern can be attributed to the compounding effect of sustained non-English generation:\n1879\tTranslation→Summarization models must first\n1880\ttranslate the entire long-form document into the target language before condensing it, requiring long,\n1881\tsustained generation in the non-English target and\n1882\tincreasing the opportunity for language-specific errors to accumulate before summarization even begins. In contrast, for Summarization→Translation,\n1883\tthe model first condenses the content in English,\n1884\tits likely dominant pretraining language, and only\n1885\t\n1886\t\fFigure 7: Named entities count between reference and predicted summaries. Entities predicted by Spacy.\n1887\t\n1888\tFigure 8: NER average count difference between\n1889\treference and predicted summary for each model.\n1890\t\n1891\tafterward performs the comparatively shorter task\n1892\tof translating a brief summary, limiting the extent\n1893\tof non-English generation and constraining error\n1894\taccumulation.\n1895\t\n1896\t6\n1897\t\n1898\tDiscussion\n1899\t\n1900\tIn order to further analyze what characterizes\n1901\thigher-quality summaries, we conduct several complementary analyses. We first perform human annotation across the generated summaries of different models (Figure 5, Appendix B), which\n1902\treveals a consistent ranking of Gemini3.1-Pro,\n1903\tQwen3-Omni, then Gemma4-12B, and aligns with\n1904\tall previous metric-based results. To better understand what drives this ranking, we conduct a\n1905\tmacro-level analysis of summary length (Figure 6).\n1906\tWe find that Gemini3.1-Pro and Qwen3-Omni consistently generate longer summaries than the reference ground truth, while Gemma4-12B produces\n1907\tshorter summaries than the reference across all\n1908\tprompting methods except CoT, where its output\n1909\tis only marginally longer than the reference. This\n1910\tpattern suggests a possible association between\n1911\tsummary length and perceived quality, with longer\n1912\tsummaries corresponding to higher-ranked models.\n1913\tTo test this hypothesis more directly, we move\n1914\tbeyond macro-level length statistics to a microlevel analysis of summary content, examining\n1915\tthe specific entities each model includes through\n1916\t\n1917\tnamed entity recognition (NER) using SpaCy\n1918\t(en_core_web_sm) on the English output and reference only, for consistency. As shown in Figure\n1919\t8, this finer-grained analysis reinforces the lengthbased pattern: Gemini3.1-Pro and Qwen3-Omni\n1920\tinclude more named entities than the reference\n1921\tacross the ZS and CoT settings and a similar amont\n1922\tin the FS setting, while Gemma4-12B includes a\n1923\tsimilar amount of named entities as the reference\n1924\tin the ZS and FS settings, and more in CoT. Entity category analysis (Figure 7) reveals that this\n1925\ttrend holds nearly uniformly across entity types\n1926\t(e.g., Geopolitical Entity (GPE), individual names\n1927\t(PERSON), Organization (ORG), and NORP (Nationalities, Other groups, Religious groups, Political groups)); Gemini3.1-Pro and Qwen3-Omni surface more entities than the reference in almost every\n1928\tcategory, while Gemma4-12B demonstrates fewer.\n1929\t\n1930\t7\n1931\t\n1932\tConclusion\n1933\t\n1934\tIn this paper, we have introduced JSumT, a task\n1935\trequiring models to compress the salient content\n1936\tof a long spoken document into a summary rendered in a target language, and present VOX S UMM, the first multilingual and cross-lingual\n1937\tbenchmark for long-form speech summarization\n1938\tspanning 24 languages. Our evaluation reveals substantial variation: Gemini3.1-Pro performs best,\n1939\tfew-shot prompting benefits stronger models, and\n1940\tgenerating English summaries from non-English\n1941\tspeech is typically better than the reverse. Moreover, translating before summarizing amplifies\n1942\tinstruction-following failures, making summarizethen-translate the more robust pipeline. Although\n1943\thigh-resource languages outperform low-resource\n1944\tones on average, this pattern breaks down at the\n1945\tindividual-language level (e.g., Korean and Portuguese perform worse than lower-resourced Kyrgyz and Thai). Overall, these findings establish\n1946\tJSumT as a challenging task, and we hope VOX S UMM and these findings provide a foundation for\n1947\tbuilding more robust and inclusive multilingual and\n1948\t\n1949\t\fcross-lingual speech summarization systems.\n1950\t\n1951\t8\n1952\t\n1953\tLimitations\n1954\t\n1955\tVOX S UMM is derived from the CrossSum dataset,\n1956\twhose cross-lingual pairs are identified automatically using semantic similarity between summaries.\n1957\tAs such, this may reflect imperfect alignment, however, we mitigate this by retaining only complete\n1958\tinstances, reconstructing bidirectional pairs conservatively, and applying quality-control checks during dataset construction. Additionally, the speech\n1959\tin VOX S UMM is generated from professionally\n1960\twritten news text using a multilingual TTS system rather than collected from naturally occurring\n1961\tbroadcasts. Despite this, CER, NISQA, and human\n1962\tassessments indicate that the generated audio is\n1963\tintelligible and of high perceptual quality, making\n1964\tit suitable for controlled comparison across languages.\n1965\t\n1966\t9\n1967\t\n1968\tEthical considerations\n1969\t\n1970\tThis project involved collecting annotated data\n1971\tfrom 10 languages (out of 24) to verify the quality\n1972\tof the generated speech data. Additional annotations were collected for validating LLM-as-a-Judge\n1973\tevaluation. All participants were fairly compensated for their contributions at $9 per hour, on Upwork annotation platform.\n1974\t\n1975\t10\n1976\t\n1977\tAcknowledgement\n1978\t\n1979\tThis research was supported in part by the Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council\n1980\t(NSERC) of Canada and in part by the AI2050 program at Schmidt Sciences. This work was partially\n1981\tsupported through LLM API credits provided by\n1982\tGoogle’s Gemini Academic Program Award and\n1983\tthe OpenAI Researcher Access Award. Finally, we\n1984\tare grateful for the support from IVADO and the\n1985\tCanada First Research Excellence Fund.\n1986\t\n1987\tReferences\n1988\tEstabraq Abdulreda Kadhim, Mohammad-Reza FeiziDerakhshi, and Hadi S. Aghdasi. 2025. Advanced\n1989\ttext summarization model incorporating nlp techniques and feature-based scoring. IEEE Access,\n1990\t13:19302–19319.\n1991\tDavid Ifeoluwa Adelani, Victor Agostinelli, Antonios\n1992\tAnastasopoulos, Luisa Bentivogli, Ondřej Bojar,\n1993\tSébastien Bratières, Marine Carpuat, Fabrício Carraro, Roldano Cattoni, Mauro Cettolo, Lizhong Chen,\n1994\tMarcello Federico, Marco Gaido, Mahendra Gupta,\n1995\t\n1996\tHyoJung Han, Ali Hatami, Lewis C. Howe, Dávid Javorský, Yejin Jeon, and 36 others. 2026. Speech\n1997\ttranslation and metrics in 2026: Findings of the\n1998\tIWSLT campaign. In Proceedings of the 23rd International Conference on Spoken Language Translation (IWSLT 2026), pages 336–422, San Diego,\n1999\tUSA (in-person and online). Association for Computational Linguistics.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "714b59ad-b3aa-4f8f-8fbc-b53183577fe2", "uuid": "9e1da706-ac91-4766-9dd5-2caddfa3833a", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T09:10:43.644Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/voxsumm.txt", "content": "0.675\n\n0.690\n\n0.588\n\n0.595\n\n0.597\n\n0.593\n\n0.596\n\n0.629\n\n0.596\n\n0.607\n\n(0.400)\n\n(0.278)