{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-af02b729887502da", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2600\tTable 6: Influence of input modality on performance.\n2601\tBERTScore results between Qwen3-Omni FS text and\n2602\taudio article inputs.\n2603\t\n2604\tA.2\n2605\t\n2606\tExperimental Details and Results\n2607\t\n2608\tWhile Gemini3.1-Pro and Gemma4-12B officially\n2609\tsupport all 24 languages included in our benchmark, Qwen3-Omni lacks official support for some\n2610\tof the low-resource languages: Amharic, Bengali,\n2611\tGujarati, Hindi, Kyrgyz, Persian, Punjabi, Sinhala,\n2612\tSwahili, Tamil, Telugu, Thai, Ukrainian and Vietnamese. However, we do not exclude them for our\n2613\tevaluation of Qwen3-Omni, as preliminary analysis showed that it still performed relatively well,\n2614\tparticularly in CoT and FS settings.\n2615\tCascading Pipeline To further assess the performance of actual cascaded systems, we construct the following pipeline: Omnilingual-ASR\n2616\t(LLM-1B) first transcribes the source audio, after which Gemini3.1-Pro summarizes the resulting transcript; the summary is then passed to\n2617\ta separate instance of Gemini3.1-Pro, which\n2618\ttranslates it into the target language. We apply\n2619\tthe same cascaded procedure in reverse for the\n2620\tTranslation→Summarization setting. As shown in\n2621\tTable 5, this cascaded pipeline degrades Gemini’s\n2622\tperformance relative to its corresponding end-toend systems in Table 2, regardless of whether summarization or translation is performed first. This\n2623\tdegradation underscores the substantial impact of\n2624\terror propagation across the successive ASR, trans-\n2625\t\n2626\t\fFigure 9: Human annotation and G-Eval correlation per\n2627\tlanguage. Note that Fluency is ranked from 1 to 3,\n2628\tmirroring (Liu et al., 2023).\n2629\t\n2630\tlation, and summarization stages inherent to cascaded architectures.\n2631\tModality Ablation In order to isolate the impact\n2632\tof acoustic encoding on JSumT performance, we\n2633\tevaluate Qwen3-Omni under a text-only FS setting\n2634\tacross all languages, substituting the synthesized\n2635\taudio input with the original source-language article text (Table 6). We observe a consistent improvement across all languages under text input,\n2636\twith an average BERTScore-F1 gain of +0.017 relative to the audio-based setting. This indicates that\n2637\tJSumT’s difficulty is not confined to cross-lingual\n2638\tgeneration alone, but also stems from information\n2639\tloss introduced during acoustic encoding.\n2640\tFurthermore, in order to compare the difficulty of speech summarization versus speech\n2641\tJSumT, we evaluate Qwen3-Omni in a FS Englishto-English summarization setting. We observe\n2642\tan improvement over the average Qwen3-Omni\n2643\tFS Summarization→Translation (0.665) in the\n2644\tsummarization-only task (0.700), which is a 0.035\n2645\tgain in BERTScore-F1 performance.\n2646\t\n2647\tB\n2648\t\n2649\tHuman Evaluation Protocol\n2650\t\n2651\tEach human annotator is hired through a verified\n2652\tfreelancer hiring platform (Upwork) and asked to\n2653\tprovide a short spoken introduction in both English\n2654\tand the target language, in order to verify fluency\n2655\tin both. Three annotators are hired per language\n2656\tand compensated $45 USD for completing a 50question quality survey.\n2657\tAdditionally, a separate evaluation spanning two\n2658\thigh-resource languages (French, Korean) and one\n2659\tlow-resource language (Amharic) is conducted in\n2660\torder to further investigate summary construction\n2661\t\n2662\tquality and the correlation between human annotators and G-Eval. For each studied language, three\n2663\tannotators through the same platform are hired, and\n2664\teach are compensated $100 USD. 100 summaries\n2665\tthat are generated by the best-performing model,\n2666\tGemini3.1-Pro, are randomly selected across a\n2667\trange of experimental configurations (Trans-Sum\n2668\tFS/ZS, Sum-Trans FS/ZS, and CoT). Following the\n2669\tG-Eval protocol, annotators are first asked to listen\n2670\tto the entire audio article, after which they rate a\n2671\tsingle proposed summary from 1 to 5 according\n2672\tto four criteria: Coherence, Fluency (1-3 scale),\n2673\tConsistency, and Relevance. An example survey\n2674\tquestion can be found in Figure 27.\n2675\tAs shown in Figure 9, high-resource languages\n2676\ttend to outperform the low-resource language;\n2677\tnonetheless, all three languages achieve high ratings across all four criteria, indicating strong summary quality regardless of resource level. Correlation between automatic G-Eval scores and human\n2678\tevaluations is high, with an overall Pearson correlation of 0.98, further validating G-Eval as a reliable\n2679\tproxy for human judgment in this setting.\n2680\t\n2681\tC\n2682\t\n2683\tHuman Evaluation Analyses\n2684\t\n2685\tTo assess rater consistency, we compute interannotator variance, or “disagreement\": the standard deviation of scores given by independent annotators to the same audio sample. Lower values\n2686\tindicate stronger agreement; a standard deviation\n2687\tof zero means all annotators scored the item identically. See Figures 10a to 10j.\n2688\t\n2689\t\f(a) Amharic\n2690\t\n2691\t(b) Chinese\n2692\t\n2693\t(c) French\n2694\t\n2695\t(d) Gujarati\n2696\t\n2697\t(e) Korean\n2698\t\n2699\t(f) Portuguese\n2700\t\n2701\t(g) Spanish\n2702\t\n2703\t(h) Swahili\n2704\t\n2705\t(i) Tamil\n2706\t\n2707\t(j) Ukrainian\n2708\t\n2709\tFigure 10: Inter-annotator (IA) variance for quality estimation across languages.\n2710\t\n2711\t\fGemini3.1-Pro\n2712\t\n2713\tPair\n2714\tam\n2715\tar\n2716\tbn\n2717\tzh\n2718\tfr\n2719\tgu\n2720\thi\n2721\tid\n2722\tja\n2723\tko\n2724\tky\n2725\tfa\n2726\tpt\n2727\tpa\n2728\tru\n2729\tsi\n2730\tes\n2731\tsw\n2732\tta\n2733\tte\n2734\tth\n2735\ttr\n2736\tuk\n2737\tvi\n2738\tAvg\n2739\t\n2740\tGemma4-12B\